#{ ===========================================================================
   DESTRUCTION CANNOT FAIL -- the philosophy (11.4, R-36).

   A disposal work declares NO signals. This is not an omission; it is the
   keystone that keeps the ownership law (0.3) dischargeable:

   1. THE DEBT MUST BE PAYABLE. "Give or destruct what you have" is a duty
      on EVERY path -- including the fault paths. A haver inside its own
      exit: cascade must still discharge; if destructing could fail, the
      haver would need a handler inside the handler, and THAT failure a
      handler in turn -- the regress never grounds. Total destruction
      grounds it: the last resort that cannot refuse.

   2. FAILURE-DURING-FAILURE CASCADES. The classic ruin: an error path
      releases its resources, a release errs, and the original fault is
      shadowed by the cleanup's fault. With signal-less disposal the
      original fault is the ONLY fault -- the trace stays readable.

   3. TROUBLE DURING RELEASE IS INFORMATION, NOT REFUSAL. A close() that
      errs deep below does not resurrect the debt -- the having HAS ended.
      What remains is a fact worth reporting: an EVENT on the bus (the
      ephemeral channel exists precisely for occurrences that nobody owns,
      explain-ephemerals.vut) -- never a signal that re-obligates the
      caller of the end itself.

   The checker holds the law: a '-class' completion with a signals:
   section is REJECTED (SEMANTICS 25) -- locked in the suite's
   work_rejects choice.
   =========================================================================== }

lease : class has: { held: float; } {
    from : work
}

+lease.from : work(knows: units
                   gives: l : lease
                   signals: exhausted) {
    if: units > 100 { exit: exhausted; }    # ACQUISITION may fail -- and
    l = units;                              # says so: that is its job
    finish:
}

#{ The mirror is asymmetric ON PURPOSE: taking the world's resources can
   be refused; handing them back cannot. The panel spells the asymmetry --
   gives-and-signals above, takes-and-silence below. }

-lease : work(takes: l : lease) {
    # were the release to err deep below, this body would EMIT an event
    # (occurrence, unowned) -- the caller's obligation is already met the
    # moment custody arrived here.
    finish:
}

Demo : behavior has: { got: float; } {
    SHOW => {
        b.got = lease.from(units = 25) else: { exhausted => { b.got = 0; } }
    }
}
